 extended formulations that rely on auxiliary variables and constraints, utilizing only the limited set of standard cones supported by sophisticated solvers like MOSEK 9. However, these extended forms are frequently far larger and more intricate than equivalent conic natural formulations, which can leverage a wider variety of "exotic" cones. We classify a cone as exotic if it is a proper cone for which we can develop tractable logarithmically homogeneous self-concordant barrier oracles for either the cone or its dual. This paper presents Hypatia, a highly configurable, open-source conic primal-dual interior point solver featuring a generic interface for exotic cones. Written in Julia and accessible via JuMP, Hypatia currently supports approximately two dozen predefined useful cones, including several variants. We define specific exotic cones within Hypatia and analyze methods for constructing equivalent representations using standard cones for constraints involving these exotic cones. For problems across various applications, we propose natural formulations utilizing these exotic cones, demonstrating that they are both simpler and lower-dimensional than their equivalent extended counterparts. Our computational results highlight the potential benefits—particularly regarding solve time and memory efficiency—of addressing natural formulations with Hypatia compared to solving extended formulations with either Hypatia or MOSEK 9. We believe Hypatia serves as a valuable platform for researching formulations and constraints across diverse applications. As users identify new cones and constraints, Hypatia is designed to facilitate this process efficiently. Hypatia is released under an Apache 2.0 license. The software is available at https://github.com/grapah/hypatia.

